<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suraj Mal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Maharaja Suraj Mal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suraj Mal (1707–1763) was the Jat ruler of Bharatpur who turned his family's holdings west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> into a powerful kingdom. He built the fortress of Lohagarh at Bharatpur, took Agra in 1761 and profited from the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> defeat at Panipat. He was killed in battle against the Rohilla chief Najib ad-Dawla near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1763.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suraj-mal.jpg" alt="Suraj Mal" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Raja_Surajmal_of_Bharatpur_(1707%E2%80%931763).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Suraj Mal was born Sujan Singh on 13 February 1707, the son of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badan-singh">Badan Singh</a> and Rani Devki, in a Hindu Jat family of the Sinsinwar clan of Bharatpur. He was trained in war from a young age. His father, who was going blind, handed him power over the state in 1750, and he succeeded him officially as maharaja in 1756. He also built the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deeg">Deeg</a>, 32 km from Bharatpur, as a summer resort for the rulers of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first independent decision in external affairs came in November 1745, when Nawab Fateh Ali Khan of Koil asked his help against the Afghan chief Asad Khan, sent by the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah. Suraj Mal sent an army under his son, and the war at Chandaus ended in Asad Khan's death and the defeat of the imperial army. In the succession struggle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> he backed Ishwari Singh with 10,000 soldiers, and Ishwari Singh won the battle of August 1748.</p><p>He fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a>, the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a>, when Safdar Jang attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1754 Khanderao Holkar, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-holkar">Malhar Rao Holkar</a>, besieged his town of Kumher for the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii">Alamgir II</a>; Khanderao was killed by a cannonball while inspecting his troops, the siege was lifted and a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> followed. When the Afghan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> came to Delhi and demanded tribute in 1760, Suraj Mal pleaded poverty, and Durrani besieged his fort of Dig on 6 February 1760 before giving up and marching away.</p><p>Around March 1756 his troops took Alwar Fort from Madho Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. His capture of Agra Fort on 12 June 1761 came after a siege of a month and a bribe to its commander. After the Battle of Panipat in 1761 he sent his sons Jawahar Singh and Nahar Singh to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>. Besides the garrisons of his forts, he had an army of more than 75,000 infantry and more than 38,000 cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohilla Afghans</a> of Najib ud-Daula, an ambush near the Hindon River took his army by surprise, and Suraj Mal was killed on the night of 25 December 1763. His son and successor Jawahar Singh built his large cenotaph at Kusum Sarovar in Govardhan, flanked by those of his wives Hansiya and Kishori, its ceilings painted with scenes of the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and of Suraj Mal. His court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> recorded his life in the Sujan Charitra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suraj Mal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Suraj Mal</b> (ruled 1755–63) turned the chiefship into a major power, building the Lohagarh fort and capturing Agra in 1761.</blockquote><a href="/state/bharatpur-state">Bharatpur State</a></li></ul></section>
